def sec(title, body):
    return f"<section><h2>{title}</h2>{body}</section>"

page = f"""<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Topic 21 — Differentiated Services (DiffServ)</title><style>{CSS}</style></head><body>
<header><span class="tag">COMPUTER NETWORKS · UNIT 2</span><h1>21. Differentiated Services (DiffServ)</h1><p>Visual study notes explaining class-based QoS, packet marking, per-hop behaviour, and how DiffServ differs from IntServ.</p></header><main>
<div class="callout"><strong>Study-source note:</strong> These notes explain the standard networking concept. Match the exact terminology and depth to your prescribed Unit 2 PPT; any illustrative examples below are labelled as such.</div>
{sec("1. Learning objectives", """<ul><li>Define Differentiated Services (DiffServ).</li><li>Explain traffic classification, marking, and forwarding treatment.</li><li>Understand the DS field and Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP).</li><li>Describe Per-Hop Behaviour (PHB) and traffic conditioning.</li><li>Compare DiffServ with Integrated Services (IntServ).</li></ul>""")}
{sec("2. What is DiffServ?", """<p><strong>Differentiated Services (DiffServ)</strong> is a network architecture for providing different levels of service to traffic classes. Instead of maintaining reservation state for every individual flow throughout the network, packets are classified into classes and receive treatment based on their markings and network policies.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Core idea:</strong> classify traffic → mark packets → apply the configured treatment to each class at routers.</div><p>DiffServ can support QoS objectives, but a marking by itself does not guarantee a particular delay, bandwidth, or loss rate. The network must configure and provision the corresponding behaviour.</p>""")}
{sec("3. Why use DiffServ?", """<ul><li>Large networks may carry huge numbers of concurrent flows.</li><li>Maintaining reservation state separately for every flow can be expensive.</li><li>Traffic classes allow routers to handle packets in aggregates.</li><li>Administrators can define policies for which traffic receives higher or different priority.</li></ul><div class="diagram"><div class="flow"><div class="box">Many application flows</div><span class="arrow">→</span><div class="box">Classify &amp; mark</div><span class="arrow">→</span><div class="box">Traffic classes</div><span class="arrow">→</span><div class="box">Class-based forwarding</div></div><p class="muted">Conceptual diagram: multiple flows are grouped into traffic classes for forwarding treatment.</p></div>""")}
{sec("4. Main components", """<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Traffic classification</h3><p>Identify packets using configured criteria, such as addresses, protocol, ports, or existing markings.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Packet marking</h3><p>Set the packet's DSCP to indicate the intended forwarding class.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Traffic conditioning</h3><p>At network boundaries, policies may meter, shape, police, or remark traffic to keep it within an agreed profile.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Per-Hop Behaviour (PHB)</h3><p>Each router applies the configured forwarding treatment associated with the packet's class.</p></div>
</div>""")}
{sec("5. DS field and DSCP", """<p>In IP packets, the Differentiated Services field contains the <strong>Differentiated Services Code Point (DSCP)</strong>, which is used to select a per-hop behaviour. DSCP is six bits wide, allowing 64 code-point values. The remaining two bits in the IPv4 Differentiated Services field are used for Explicit Congestion Notification (ECN), when ECN is used.</p>
<div class="diagram"><div class="flow"><div class="box">IP header</div><span class="arrow">→</span><div class="box">DS field<br><strong>6-bit DSCP</strong><br>2-bit ECN</div><span class="arrow">→</span><div class="box">Router selects<br>configured PHB</div></div></div>
<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> DSCP is a marking in the packet. PHB is the forwarding behaviour configured for that marking in a network. The same marking should be interpreted consistently within the relevant administrative domain.</p>""")}
{sec("6. Per-Hop Behaviour (PHB)", """<p>A <strong>Per-Hop Behaviour</strong> describes the externally observable packet-forwarding treatment a DiffServ node applies to a traffic aggregate. It may affect how packets are queued, scheduled, or dropped during congestion.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Common PHB category</th><th>General purpose</th><th>Exam point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Default PHB</strong></td><td>Ordinary forwarding treatment when no special class treatment is selected.</td><td>Comparable to conventional best-effort treatment, depending on configuration.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Expedited Forwarding (EF)</strong></td><td>Designed for a low-loss, low-delay, low-jitter service when properly provisioned.</td><td>Often associated with voice-like real-time traffic; it is not an automatic guarantee.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Assured Forwarding (AF)</strong></td><td>Defines classes and drop precedence to differentiate forwarding treatment and congestion-drop likelihood.</td><td>Packets can receive different drop treatment under congestion.</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="muted">These are standard DiffServ concepts. Learn specific DSCP values only if your syllabus or PPT requires them.</p>""")}
{sec("7. How DiffServ works", """<ol><li><strong>Traffic enters a network domain.</strong> An edge router identifies the traffic using the network's policy.</li><li><strong>Packets are classified.</strong> Traffic is assigned to an appropriate aggregate or class.</li><li><strong>Packets are marked.</strong> The router sets or checks the DSCP according to policy.</li><li><strong>Traffic may be conditioned.</strong> A meter checks whether traffic matches its profile; shaping, policing, or remarking may be applied.</li><li><strong>Core routers read the marking.</strong> They apply the configured PHB, for example by using class-specific queues and scheduling.</li><li><strong>Traffic exits the domain.</strong> The next network may preserve or re-evaluate markings according to its policy.</li></ol><div class="diagram"><div class="flow"><div class="box">Ingress edge<br>classify / mark</div><span class="arrow">→</span><div class="box">Condition traffic<br>if needed</div><span class="arrow">→</span><div class="box">Core routers<br>apply PHB</div><span class="arrow">→</span><div class="box">Egress edge</div></div></div>""")}
{sec("8. Illustrative example", """<p>A campus network carries voice calls, video meetings, and large software downloads. The network administrator configures three traffic classes:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Traffic</th><th>Possible class policy</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voice packets</td><td>Low-delay class with carefully controlled capacity</td><td>Voice is sensitive to delay and jitter.</td></tr><tr><td>Interactive video</td><td>Real-time class with appropriate bandwidth and queue treatment</td><td>Supports smoother interactive communication.</td></tr><tr><td>Bulk downloads</td><td>Standard or lower-priority class</td><td>Usually tolerates more variation in completion time.</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="muted">Illustrative policy only—not a universal DSCP mapping. Actual markings and queue configurations depend on the network administrator's policy.</p>""")}
{sec("9. Advantages and limitations", """<div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>✅ Advantages</h3><ul><li>Scales better than per-flow reservation in many large networks.</li><li>Routers can operate on aggregates of traffic.</li><li>Supports policy-based differentiation between classes.</li><li>Can work across managed network domains with compatible policies.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><h3>⚠️ Limitations</h3><ul><li>A DSCP marking alone does not guarantee QoS.</li><li>Requires consistent policies and capacity planning.</li><li>Mis-marked traffic can abuse preferred classes unless controlled.</li><li>Service quality can vary across domains with different configurations.</li></ul></div></div>""")}
{sec("10. DiffServ vs. IntServ", """<table><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>DiffServ</th><th>IntServ</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Granularity</td><td>Traffic class / aggregate</td><td>Individual flow</td></tr><tr><td>Router state</td><td>Generally class-based treatment in the core</td><td>Per-flow reservation state for admitted flows</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Classification, DSCP marking, PHB and conditioning</td><td>Reservation signalling and admission control</td></tr><tr><td>Scalability</td><td>Typically more scalable for large aggregates</td><td>Per-flow state can be costly at scale</td></tr><tr><td>Primary strength</td><td>Manageable differentiated treatment across many flows</td><td>Explicit per-flow resource control</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="good"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> DiffServ = class-based differentiation; IntServ = per-flow reservation.</div>""")}
{sec("11. Exam-ready answers", """<div class="marks"><h3>3 marks — Define DiffServ.</h3><p>Differentiated Services (DiffServ) is a QoS architecture that groups packets into traffic classes. Packets are classified and marked using the DSCP field, and routers apply a configured Per-Hop Behaviour (PHB) to each class. It avoids the need to maintain reservation state for every flow in the core network.</p></div>
<div class="marks"><h3>5 marks — Explain the working of DiffServ.</h3><ol><li>Traffic arrives at an edge router.</li><li>The router classifies traffic according to configured rules.</li><li>Packets are marked with a DSCP value.</li><li>Traffic conditioning, such as policing or shaping, may enforce the traffic profile.</li><li>Core routers use the marking to select the configured PHB, which determines the forwarding treatment.</li></ol></div>
<div class="marks"><h3>8–10 marks — Explain DiffServ architecture with a diagram.</h3><p>Define DiffServ and explain the need for class-based QoS. Draw ingress edge → traffic conditioning → core routers applying PHB → egress edge. Describe classification, DSCP marking, conditioning, and PHB. Discuss default, EF and AF categories if included in your syllabus. Conclude with advantages, limitations, and a comparison with IntServ.</p></div>""")}
{sec("12. Practice questions", """<div class="q"><strong>Q1. Expand DiffServ and DSCP.</strong><p class="answer">DiffServ: Differentiated Services. DSCP: Differentiated Services Code Point.</p></div><div class="q"><strong>Q2. What does PHB mean?</strong><p class="answer">Per-Hop Behaviour—the forwarding treatment applied by a router to a traffic aggregate.</p></div><div class="q"><strong>Q3. How many bits are in the DSCP?</strong><p class="answer">6 bits.</p></div><div class="q"><strong>Q4. Why is DiffServ scalable?</strong><p class="answer">It generally handles aggregates/classes instead of maintaining per-flow reservation state in every core router.</p></div><div class="q"><strong>Q5. Does marking a packet with a high-priority DSCP guarantee low delay?</strong><p class="answer">No. Appropriate configuration, provisioning, and policy enforcement are also required.</p></div><div class="q"><strong>Q6. Give one difference between EF and AF PHBs.</strong><p class="answer">EF is designed for low-delay, low-loss treatment under suitable provisioning; AF provides classes and drop precedence to differentiate forwarding and drop treatment.</p></div>""")}
{sec("13. Quick revision", """<ul><li><strong>DiffServ:</strong> class-based QoS architecture.</li><li><strong>DSCP:</strong> 6-bit code point in the IP Differentiated Services field.</li><li><strong>PHB:</strong> forwarding behaviour associated with a class/marking.</li><li><strong>Edge router:</strong> typically classifies, marks, and conditions traffic.</li><li><strong>Core router:</strong> applies configured class-based treatment.</li><li><strong>EF:</strong> designed for low-delay, low-loss treatment when provisioned correctly.</li><li><strong>AF:</strong> uses classes and drop precedence.</li><li><strong>Main strength:</strong> scalable aggregate treatment.</li><li><strong>Main caution:</strong> markings alone do not create a QoS guarantee.</li></ul><div class="good"><strong>Exam checklist:</strong> practise the architecture diagram, define DSCP and PHB accurately, and state the main difference from IntServ.</div>""")}
</main><footer>CN Notes · Unit 2 · Topic 21 — Differentiated Services (DiffServ)</footer></body></html>"""

OUT.write_text(page, encoding="utf-8")
print(f"Created: {OUT}")
